M("LPN scope",4,"A stable client needs a wound irrigation that the LPN has performed before, a new admission assessment, and discharge teaching that has not been started. An RN and an LPN are on the team.","Which task may the RN assign to the LPN?","The new admission assessment","The first discharge teaching","The familiar wound irrigation on the stable client","The care plan for a client whose condition just changed","The familiar wound irrigation on the stable client","An LPN may perform a known procedure on a stable client. New assessments, first teaching, and an unstable plan stay with the RN.","Assigning the admission, the first teaching, or the changing client gives the LPN work that requires the RN's assessment.",R)
M("UAP and a new symptom",5,"Assistive personnel report that a client who was walking an hour ago now says the chest feels tight. The nurse is in another room passing medications that are not time-critical.","What is the nurse's best direction?","Finish the medication pass, then assess the chest tightness","Ask the assistive person to take vital signs and report back in 30 minutes","Stop and assess the client with chest tightness now","Tell the assistive person to have the client take a nitroglycerin tablet","Stop and assess the client with chest tightness now","A new report of chest tightness is a nurse assessment, not a task to postpone or delegate.","Finishing the pass, delaying vital signs, or having assistive personnel give nitroglycerin skips or misplaces the assessment.",R)
M("ethical advocacy",4,"A client does not understand tomorrow's surgery. The surgeon is still in the hospital. The client asks the nurse to advocate so the decision is not made in confusion.","Which action advocates for the client?","Tell the client to sign so the operating room schedule is kept","Call the surgeon to return and speak with the client before consent","Ask a roommate to explain the surgery","Document that the client seemed nervous and send the client anyway","Call the surgeon to return and speak with the client before consent","Advocacy here is arranging the person who can explain the procedure before consent.","Keeping the schedule, using a roommate, or sending a confused client does not protect an informed decision.",R)
M("mandatory reporting",5,"An older client has bruises in different stages on both upper arms and says a caregiver grabs them during arguments. The client asks the nurse not to tell anyone.","What does the nurse do?","Promise secrecy because the client requested it","Report the suspected abuse as required and tell the client what must be reported","Wait until the client agrees in writing","Document the bruises and discharge the client with the caregiver","Report the suspected abuse as required and tell the client what must be reported","Suspected abuse of a vulnerable adult is reported. The nurse does not promise a secrecy that the law does not allow.","Secrecy, waiting for written consent, or sending the client home with the caregiver leaves the risk unreported.",R)
M("priority after report",4,"Report includes a client with a potassium of 6.4 mEq/L and peaked T waves, a client who needs a routine colostomy bag change, a client requesting a sleeping pill at 2100, and a client whose family wants an update.","Whom does the nurse see first?","The client who wants a sleeping pill","The client with potassium 6.4 mEq/L and peaked T waves","The family waiting for an update","The client needing a colostomy bag change","The client with potassium 6.4 mEq/L and peaked T waves","Peaked T waves with severe hyperkalemia can progress to a lethal rhythm and come before routine care and updates.","A sleeping pill, a family update, and a bag change are not the immediate threat.",R)
M("assignment of an unstable client",5,"A new graduate RN, an experienced RN, an LPN, and assistive personnel are on duty. One client was just started on a heparin infusion for a pulmonary embolism and has new shortness of breath.","Who should receive that client?","The assistive personnel","The LPN","The experienced RN","The new graduate working alone without a preceptor at the bedside","The experienced RN","A client with a new heparin infusion and worsening breathing needs the experienced RN's judgment.","Assistive personnel and the LPN cannot manage this instability, and an unsupervised new graduate should not carry it alone.",R)
M("translator for consent",4,"A client who speaks little English is asked to sign a consent. The adult child offers to interpret the surgeon's risk discussion. A video interpreter is available in two minutes.","Which interpreter does the nurse arrange?","The adult child, to save time","The qualified video interpreter","A bilingual housekeeper passing in the hall","No interpreter, using gestures","The qualified video interpreter","Procedure consent needs a qualified interpreter. A child or an untrained staff member is not that role.","Using the child, a passerby, or gestures risks an inaccurate consent.",R)
M("DNR and surgery",5,"A capacitated client with a do-not-resuscitate order is going to the operating room for a palliative procedure. The anesthesiologist has not discussed the order.","What does the nurse support?","Automatically suspend the order without talking to the client","Review the order with the client and the perioperative team and document the agreed plan","Cancel the order because surgery is resuscitation","Ask the family to decide because the client is going to sleep","Review the order with the client and the perioperative team and document the agreed plan","Perioperative code status is reconsidered with the client, not silently suspended or cancelled.","Automatic suspension, treating surgery as a cancelled order, or handing the choice to the family skips the client's decision.",R)
M("resource triage on the unit",4,"Two clients call at once. One has a new facial droop and arm weakness that started 20 minutes ago. The other needs help to the bathroom and has been waiting 10 minutes.","Whom does the nurse see first?","The client who needs the bathroom","The client with the new facial droop and arm weakness","Whichever client called first","The client closest to the nurses' station if that is the bathroom call","The client with the new facial droop and arm weakness","Sudden focal deficits are a stroke emergency. Toileting can be covered by another team member.","Order of the call or proximity does not outrank a possible stroke.",R)
M("social media",3,"A nurse wants to post a de-identified story about a rare diagnosis from today's shift, including the hospital unit and the day.","Which action is appropriate?","Post it because the name was removed","Do not post client information from the shift","Post it to a private staff group","Post the photo of the monitor with the name cropped","Do not post client information from the shift","Unit, date, and a rare story can identify a client. Removing the name is not enough.","A private group or a cropped monitor photo can still be a disclosure.",R)

# Safety
SF("airborne precautions",5,"A client has fever, cough, and a rash that started on the face. Measles is suspected. The client is in a shared semi-private room.","Which placement does the nurse arrange?","A shared room with a surgical mask on the client only","A private airborne-infection isolation room and a respirator for staff entering","Contact precautions in the current room","Droplet precautions with the door open","A private airborne-infection isolation room and a respirator for staff entering","Measles is airborne. The client needs airborne isolation and staff need a respirator, not a surgical mask alone.","A shared room, contact precautions, or an open door do not contain airborne virus.",C)
SF("droplet precautions",4,"A client is admitted with suspected meningococcal meningitis. The client is maskless in the hallway returning from imaging.","What does the nurse do first?","Place a surgical mask on the client and complete droplet precautions","Start airborne isolation with an N95 only","Remove precautions because the hallway trip is over","Use gloves only","Place a surgical mask on the client and complete droplet precautions","Meningococcus spreads by droplets. A surgical mask and droplet precautions are the immediate control.","Airborne isolation, ending precautions, or gloves alone miss droplet spread.",C)
SF("contact precautions",4,"A client has watery stool and a positive Clostridioides difficile test. The roommate has no symptoms.","Which practice does the nurse use?","Alcohol hand rub alone after glove removal","Soap and water after glove removal, plus contact precautions","A surgical mask as the only addition","Standard precautions without a gown","Soap and water after glove removal, plus contact precautions","C. difficile spores are not killed by alcohol gel. Soap and water and contact precautions are required.","Alcohol gel alone, a mask only, or no gown leave spores on hands and surfaces.",C)
SF("fall risk highest",4,"Four clients are on the unit. One is older, received a new opioid an hour ago, and has an urgent need to toilet. One is young and waiting for discharge teaching. One is sleeping after a physical therapy session with the bed alarm on. One is alert and using a walker independently.","Which client does the nurse help first to prevent a fall?","The client waiting for discharge teaching","The older client who received an opioid and needs to toilet","The sleeping client with the bed alarm on","The client using a walker independently","The older client who received an opioid and needs to toilet","New opioid sedation plus an urgent toilet need is the highest fall risk right now.","Teaching, a sleeping client already alarmed, and independent walker use are lower immediate risks.",J)
SF("restraints",5,"A client is pulling at a nasogastric tube. The nurse has not yet tried reorientation, a sitter, or camouflage of the tube. A coworker suggests bilateral wrist restraints.","Which action comes first?","Apply restraints so the tube stays in","Try less restrictive measures and have the nurse stay with the client","Tie the wrists to the bed with a sheet","Ask the family to hold the client down for the shift","Try less restrictive measures and have the nurse stay with the client","Restraints are a last resort after less restrictive measures fail and an order is obtained.","Restraints, a sheet, or family restraint skip safer options and the required order.",J)
SF("medication reconciliation",4,"A client takes warfarin at home. The admission list omits it. The client is about to receive a new order for ibuprofen.","What does the nurse do?","Give the ibuprofen because it is a new order","Hold the ibuprofen, clarify the home warfarin, and reconcile the list before giving a conflicting drug","Document that the client probably stopped warfarin","Substitute aspirin","Hold the ibuprofen, clarify the home warfarin, and reconcile the list before giving a conflicting drug","Reconciliation catches a high-risk drug that was left off the list before a bleeding interaction is given.","Giving ibuprofen, assuming warfarin was stopped, or substituting aspirin adds bleeding risk.",I)
SF("wrong-site time out",4,"The nurse is in the operating room. The consent says left knee, the surgical mark is on the right knee, and the client is already sedated.","What does the nurse do?","Proceed because the client is sedated and the room is ready","Stop and reconcile the consent, the mark, and the site before incision","Ask the scrub person which knee looks more arthritic","Document the mismatch after the procedure","Stop and reconcile the consent, the mark, and the site before incision","A site mismatch stops the procedure until consent and marking agree. Sedation does not waive the time-out.","Proceeding, guessing from appearance, or documenting later allows a wrong-site surgery.",J)
SF("fire response",3,"The nurse discovers a fire in a client room. A client is in the bed. The alarm has not been pulled.","Which action is first?","Search for the fire extinguisher before moving anyone","Rescue the client from the room","Close doors in the rest of the unit before rescuing","File an incident report","Rescue the client from the room","Rescue comes before alarm, confine, and extinguish when a person is in immediate danger.","Looking for an extinguisher or writing a report first leaves the client in the fire.",R)
SF("needlestick",4,"A nurse is stuck by a needle used on a client with HIV and a detectable viral load. The stick just happened. Baseline labs are not drawn yet.","What is the immediate plan?","Wash the site and start post-exposure prophylaxis without waiting for the nurse's baseline result","Wait for the baseline test before any drug","Squeeze the wound and apply alcohol only","Skip prophylaxis because the source is known","Wash the site and start post-exposure prophylaxis without waiting for the nurse's baseline result","Occupational HIV prophylaxis is started as soon as possible. Baseline labs are drawn but do not delay the first doses.","Waiting, alcohol alone, or skipping prophylaxis loses the early window.",C)
SF("suicide precautions environment",5,"A client is admitted after a suicide attempt and is on one-to-one observation. The room still has a trash-can liner, IV tubing, and a private bathroom with a lock.","Which change is required?","Allow the private locked bathroom for dignity","Remove liners, tubing, and other hazards and keep the bathroom lock from isolating the client","Check the client every 30 minutes instead of one-to-one","Leave the tubing because an IV might be needed","Remove liners, tubing, and other hazards and keep the bathroom lock from isolating the client","Observation fails if the room still contains means and a lockable space.","A locked bathroom, stretching the observation interval, or leaving tubing keeps lethal means available.",R)

